WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.590
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.400
Hi, you guys.

9
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.400
We are really excited about this week's episode.

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.680
It's kind of special.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:31.960
It really is.

12
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.000
We got to interview Mike Schur.

13
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.560
You guys know he played Moe's on the show.

14
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.600
He was also one of our original writers.

15
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.620
We got to interview him.

16
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.740
And it was awesome.

17
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.020
It was delicious.

18
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.360
We loved it.

19
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.140
We re-listened to it and we're like, this needs to be a whole episode.

20
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:47.560
Yeah.

21
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.140
We just had so much to talk with him about.

22
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.800
But first, we're still going to give you fast facts.

23
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.660
Always fast facts, guys.

24
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.220
You know.

25
00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.920
Always fast facts.

26
00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:58.740
Oh, my gosh.

27
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.180
And I feel like you're going to be like in your 80s.

28
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.760
And you're going to be like sitting in a corner mumbling like fast facts, fast facts, fast facts.

29
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:06.960
And they'll be like, what's grandma talking about?

30
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.960
Oh, she used to do this thing called fast facts.

31
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.610
Well, these are Mike Schur fast facts.

32
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.390
Fast fact number one.

33
00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.910
Mike was on the writing staff from season one through season four.

34
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.170
He wrote 10 episodes.

35
00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.710
And here's what they were.

36
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:23.810
The Alliance.

37
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:25.430
Office Olympics.

38
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:26.970
Christmas Party.

39
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:28.990
Valentine's Day.

40
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:30.950
That was one of our hidden geMs.

41
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:31.330
Yeah.

42
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:32.310
In our re-listen.

43
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.290
Branch Closing.

44
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.110
Traveling Salesman.

45
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.250
The Return.

46
00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:38.790
The Negotiation.

47
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.390
The Job.

48
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.890
That's the one where Jim comes in and asks Pam on a date at the end.

49
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:44.510
I know.

50
00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.950
And Dunder Mifflin Infinity.

51
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.350
You know, Mike told me The Negotiation was his favorite Angela Martin episode.

52
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:52.470
Oh, really?

53
00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:57.830
She's like getting titillated by hearing people recount the Jim and Roy scuffle.

54
00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:58.510
Yes.

55
00:01:58.770 --> 00:01:58.950
Yeah.

56
00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.650
He said that was his favorite episode to write for my character.

57
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.810
I felt like he just liked writing for your character a lot.

58
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.550
Your character always had great moments in his episodes.

59
00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:09.150
They did.

60
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.410
I loved it when Mike was assigned an episode.

61
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.490
Well, Mike also wrote The Accountant's Webisodes with Paul Lieberstein.

62
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:20.070
He played Moe's in 13 episodes of the show starting in season two.

63
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.230
He talks a lot about playing Moe's in our interview.

64
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.510
It's very fun.

65
00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.230
It is.

66
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.510
His love-hate relationship with Moe's.

67
00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.610
Well, here is fast fact number two.

68
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.350
I'm going to talk to you about shows he's created.

69
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.350
I think you guys know the show that he started on SNL.

70
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.990
He was there for six seasons.

71
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.650
During his time on SNL, he produced The Weekend Update.

72
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:41.870
Oh, yeah.

73
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:42.190
Yeah.

74
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:42.910
From 2001.

75
00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:43.610
That's a big deal.

76
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.150
That's a prestigious position.

77
00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:45.990
Yes.

78
00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.450
He did it from 2001 to 2004.

79
00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.090
He, of course, was a writer on The Office until 2008.

80
00:02:52.090 --> 00:03:01.310
He then went on to co-create Parks and Rec, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, and he created The Good Place and Rutherford Falls with our very own Ed HelMs.

81
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:02.210
Wow.

82
00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:02.730
Yeah.

83
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:07.450
His resume is full of other shows that he's also produced on and written for.

84
00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.530
I have to give a shout-out to one, Jenna, because I love this show.

85
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.850
Did you know that he co-produced The Comeback with Lisa Kudrow?

86
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:20.130
And he wrote two episodes, and I loved that show.

87
00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.630
I loved it.

88
00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:28.290
I found that out about him in the first season of The Office, and I was, like, enamored with him.

89
00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:36.130
Like, to me, that was, like, superstar status, that he wrote that just amazing satire.

90
00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.530
It makes me want to go back and rewatch it.

91
00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:44.150
I had forgotten that he worked on it, and when I was looking up his credits, I was like, oh, I want to rewatch The Comeback.

92
00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.410
Well, fast fact number three, Mike Schur wrote a book.

93
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:53.330
It's called How to Be Perfect, The Correct Answer to Every Moral Question.

94
00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.470
Here is how he described it to us in an email.

95
00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:57.970
He said,

96
00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:07.150
It's a book about how hard it is to be a good person and how we can use ethics and moral philosophy to try not to screw up all the time, but in a funny way,

97
00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.730
not like in a homework way.

98
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:14.330
The basic idea was to explain all the stuff I learned for The Good Place without giving everyone a headache.

99
00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:24.790
You guys, when we did this interview with Mike, our copies of his book had not arrived yet, but we have them now, and this is a truly special book.

100
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.010
I started reading it, and I can't stop.

101
00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:28.330
Same.

102
00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.330
I'm, like, binging a book.

103
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:29.830
Same.

104
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:30.890
I love it.

105
00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:31.830
I couldn't put it down.

106
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.050
I was reading it last night.

107
00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.570
This morning, I was like, I want to read more.

108
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.250
Like, I woke up thinking about it.

109
00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:36.930
Do you know what I mean?

110
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:37.190
Yeah.

111
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.490
So Mike is such an amazing writer.

112
00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.370
We already know that.

113
00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:46.730
But he takes this conversation of morality, and he makes it funny and interesting and relatable.

114
00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:53.530
For example, there's a chapter titled – and by the way, all the chapter titles are hilarious, but here's one that cracked me up.

115
00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:58.850
There's a chapter titled, Do I Have to Return My Shopping Cart to the Shopping Cart Rack Thingy?

116
00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:00.950
It's all the way over there.

117
00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:01.950
Yes.

118
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:13.230
And then, basically, what he does is he answers that question, and the answer is based on all of these ancient philosophers and, you know –

119
00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.850
Ethics and morality and your moral compass.

120
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:16.610
Yes.

121
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:23.110
So, listen, I think we should take a break, and when we come back, you get to listen to Mike Schur.

122
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.190
And we get to find out so much about Moe's.

123
00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.930
It's a treat, we promise.

124
00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:37.630
Attention all new and existing members of the WDNC.

125
00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.470
I'm Melanie Sanders, and this is the We Do Not Care Club podcaSt.

126
00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.710
We got to midlife and realized we just simply do not care much anymore.

127
00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:53.810
Now, we got to help each other make sense of hot flashes, itchy ears, chin hair, trifling families, brain fog, and bat wings.

128
00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.670
We are not alone, and we will be heard.

129
00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.590
So, come join the WDNC.

130
00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:04.130
Follow and listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcasts.

131
00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.240
Mike Schur, welcome to Office, ladies.

132
00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:09.720
Woo-hoo!

133
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.460
I'm so excited to be here.

134
00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:15.440
I'm a huge fan of you as people, first of all.

135
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:16.360
First and foremoSt.

136
00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.620
Second of all, you as actors.

137
00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.800
And then third of all, you as podcasters.

138
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:26.320
This has been such a delight to watch you do this thing to celebrate this show that we all made together.

139
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.560
I'm so happy to be here.

140
00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:28.500
It's a real honor.

141
00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.400
Oh, Mike, thank you so much.

142
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.200
We love you.

143
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.320
We're so thrilled to have you here, and I know the fans are going to enjoy just hearing your voice.

144
00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.900
Jen and I both listened to you quite a bit before this, and we both found you really pleasing to listen to.

145
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:42.580
Really?

146
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.040
Yeah, we like your voice quite a lot.

147
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:44.520
We like your voice.

148
00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.620
You listen to me in what context?

149
00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.000
Other podcasts, Mike.

150
00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:49.180
Interviews, podcasts.

151
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.360
We do our research here on Office Ladies.

152
00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:51.780
Yeah.

153
00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.360
It's very nice of you to say because I've always found my own voice to be slightly annoying,

154
00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:04.560
which I think maybe most people do, that you're – like, it's the phenomenon of hearing your voice played back to you in – this is how old we are – is, like,

155
00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.320
on an answering machine message or something.

156
00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.960
If you heard – you know, you'd be like, oh, is that what I sound like?

157
00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.020
I always feel like I sound a little annoying and nasally, but I'm glad to hear you say that.

158
00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.880
Well, I felt that way about myself, too, and I sort of brushed it off like, oh, that's just everybody feels that way about their own voice.

159
00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:31.420
But then we recently rewatched an episode where Michael is trying to get Jim to say something bad about Pam, and he gets Jim to admit that Pam's voice is rather shrill sometimes.

160
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:32.260
Yes.

161
00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:42.200
And I thought, oh, I guess maybe in the writer's room, when forced to come up with something annoying about me, we settled on my shrill voice.

162
00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.120
So thank you so much.

163
00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:44.640
That didn't give me a complex or anything.

164
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.140
Which writer hated your voice?

165
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:46.880
I don't know.

166
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.180
Mike, do you know?

167
00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:53.840
I'm very pleased to say that I think I was gone by the time that was written, so I do not have to answer for that crime.

168
00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:54.640
Well played.

169
00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:55.240
No.

170
00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:55.720
It wasn't you.

171
00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:02.940
Well, listen, we always like to ask people when they come on the podcast, how did they get their job on the office?

172
00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.120
And we know that you did double duty.

173
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.180
You were a writer, but then you were also a performer.

174
00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.620
But let's start with how did you get your job as a writer on the show?

175
00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.220
So I was writing at Saturday Night Live from 1998 to 2004.

176
00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:22.040
And in 2004, my then girlfriend, now wife of 16 years, had moved out to L.A.

177
00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:23.880
permanently in like 2002.

178
00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.340
And we were dating long distance.

179
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:34.620
And in like 2003, it was like, all right, if this is ever going to really work, either she had to move back to New York or I had to leave and move to L.A.

180
00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.220
And it made more sense for me to move to L.A.

181
00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:37.940
because there are more jobs there.

182
00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:51.060
So I gave my like year's notice to Lauren Michaels and said I was leaving at the end of the year and got an agent for the first time in my life because I never needed one.

183
00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.360
And I was like, all right, I'm moving.

184
00:08:52.360 --> 00:09:00.180
And the first thing he told me was, you know, this guy, Greg Daniels, is adapting the British office for American television.

185
00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.980
And I responded, what a terrible idea.

186
00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.640
It's just a disaster.

187
00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.220
You shouldn't do this.

188
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.700
No one should do this because I was a huge fan of the British show.

189
00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.560
This is such a classic story, right?

190
00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:13.720
I was a huge fan of the British show.

191
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.340
I thought it was absolutely brilliant.

192
00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:21.160
I didn't think there was any way he would ever work in America, but it was like, well, you know, beggars can't be choosers.

193
00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.560
Like, I have to get a job out there.

194
00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.300
So I came to L.A.

195
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:32.120
sometime in, I don't even remember, like winter, I think like February on an off week at SNL.

196
00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.560
And I had a bunch of meetings and one of them was with Greg.

197
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.760
So most of the meetings I had with producers were pretty standard hour long chats.

198
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.080
It's, you know, hey, here's our pilot, watch the pilot and let's talk about the show.

199
00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:50.780
And I dutifully tried to, you know, say nice things about whatever they were working on.

200
00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.620
And then I met with Greg and my meeting with Greg was two hours long, more than two hours long.

201
00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.280
And it was so different from every other meeting.

202
00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:06.840
It was like intense and in-depth and fascinating.

203
00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:12.000
In the middle of the meeting, he said, I'm very sorry, but my back is acting up.

204
00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.580
Would you mind if I lied down?

205
00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.180
And I said, no.

206
00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:20.540
And so he lay on the floor on his back in front of me while I sat on his couch.

207
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:29.920
So I think to an outside observer, it would look very much like we were in like Freudian analysis where I was his therapist and he was like free associating.

208
00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.520
But he just asked the most incisive questions,

209
00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:42.500
the most penetrating questions about the nature of storytelling and about the British show and why it worked and what he had to do to make it work here.

210
00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:48.320
And I left the meeting and I texted on my like Motorola Razr.

211
00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:57.680
I texted my agent and I said, I don't know if that guy's going to offer me a job, but if he does, I'm going to take it because I think he's going to teach me how to write.

212
00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.720
And my agent sends me that text like every four years back to me.

213
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.600
He sends it back to me and says like, you were right.

214
00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.000
You were right.

215
00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:07.320
You're right.

216
00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:12.680
So Greg offered me the job and I happily took it.

217
00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.340
And I thought, this is obviously not going to work.

218
00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:22.280
It is a fool's errand to try to adapt this show for an American audience.

219
00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.220
But he's going to teach me how to write.

220
00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:25.500
And that's exactly what happened.

221
00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:33.060
And that first season, you know, the only full time writers really were me, Mindy and BJ because Lester Lewis,

222
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:36.400
the late Lester Lewis and Paul Eberstein and Larry Wilmore were consultants.

223
00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:39.260
And Mindy and BJ and I had never written anything.

224
00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:40.860
We didn't have any idea what we were doing.

225
00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.100
And Greg basically taught a class.

226
00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.920
He taught like the true professor he is.

227
00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.360
He taught a class on how to write.

228
00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.940
And and it changed all of our lives forever.

229
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.300
So that's my office origin story.

230
00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:54.100
Wow.

231
00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.220
That is so good.

232
00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:58.760
Mike, I remember you guys that first season.

233
00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:00.260
There were so few of you.

234
00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:00.820
Yeah.

235
00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:04.560
And how close you all became because of that.

236
00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.780
Yeah, it was a tiny little Greg used to call it a strike force.

237
00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:16.580
He was like, this is an elite strike force, which I think was just him trying to boost our confidence because we had no idea what we were doing.

238
00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:26.540
But it was really, you know, it was it was a very, very small group of people who were working really, really hard on a very small number of episodes.

239
00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:31.060
We only had we only wrote five scripts like, you know, we each wrote.

240
00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:36.140
We actually wrote we wrote six to make five.

241
00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.340
I'm not sure if you remember this, but we wrote or even if you even knew this, Greg,

242
00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:47.940
one of the only things that Greg was able to squeeze out of NBC in that first year was he let us write one more script than we would shoot.

243
00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:52.800
And so we wrote one extra episode that I think he wrote.

244
00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:58.340
And then we just we picked the best five out of the six that we had written and threw the other one away.

245
00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.400
And I wish I could remember what that other one was.

246
00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.560
There is a there is a loSt.

247
00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.640
Oh, that'd be a good question for Greg.

248
00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:10.460
Yeah, there's a lost season one office episode script out there somewhere that we just never that we never produced.

249
00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.200
So it was and it was like, you know, it was really intense work.

250
00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:24.380
And it was a combination of of like story breaking, but also just like a masterclass in how to create television and what makes a good story.

251
00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:33.920
There were times when I've told the story before, but there are times when Greg was talking, we would be pitching and I had a notebook where I would like just jot down ideas.

252
00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:40.120
And there would be moments when Greg would say, well, let's talk about what let's just pause for a second.

253
00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:42.760
And talk about like what makes a good story.

254
00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:48.820
And then he would start kind of like going through the list of things that he felt were crucial to good TV stories.

255
00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:55.680
And I would realize like, oh, he's in lecture mode is what the way I thought of it was lecture mode, which was a good thing.

256
00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:57.680
It sounds like a bad thing, but it was a good thing.

257
00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.020
And I would turn the page and I would start taking notes like I was in college.

258
00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.640
And I would just like write down as if he were an actual professor.

259
00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.140
And I had a test coming up and I would start just writing down.

260
00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:14.800
And I still have those notes and I still go back and look at them every once in a while to like remember to refresh my memory about what he told us all about storytelling.

261
00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:20.360
It was that that intense and that sort of foundational and meaningful to all of us.

262
00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:21.180
Oh, man.

263
00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:26.240
I do remember those early days about what a small collective we were.

264
00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:27.460
Even our table reads.

265
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:27.980
Do you remember?

266
00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.400
We would just pull up chairs on the stage where we were filming.

267
00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:36.560
We would just all grab like a desk chair and sit in a circle and read the script.

268
00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.420
And I loved that.

269
00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.480
It was really intimate.

270
00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:52.940
There were so many cool things about that first season, including that the place where we worked was on this weird soundstage in the middle of Culver City or something.

271
00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:57.520
And so Hollywood soundstages are often, you know,

272
00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:06.360
these big gigantic empty rooms where you put sets and then attached to them usually or above them are a suite of offices where the writers and the production staff works.

273
00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:11.660
But that suite of offices became the actual set.

274
00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:16.540
Like the set that we built was modeled on the crappy offices where the writers were working.

275
00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:21.360
And right down to the layout, like Greg was in what would be Michael's office.

276
00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:25.320
And there was like a reception desk right outside it that became Pam's reception desk.

277
00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.360
And so we would do these drills.

278
00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:36.040
Greg would have us do these drills where he would go like, okay, take a half an hour and like go sit at Pam's desk or go sit in Michael's office.

279
00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:45.000
Or what will be Michael's office or go go sit where Jim sits and like just get a sense of like, like, what is your eyeline to Pam?

280
00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:45.660
Right.

281
00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:51.260
So he would put he would put Mindy at Pam's reception desk and he would put BJ at Jim's desk.

282
00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:55.080
And he would say, like, just sit there and like absorb the vibe of like,

283
00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:03.460
imagine that you're in love with that person and that you have to like angle your chair in such a way so that you can catch a glimpse of her out of the corner of your eye or whatever.

284
00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.440
And it was fascinating.

285
00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:06.300
It was really interesting.

286
00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:12.400
And I remember at one point he had me go into work in Michael's office or what the office that would be the model for Michael's office.

287
00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:17.400
And I remember hating it because it was like I was in this I was far away from everything.

288
00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:21.580
And all this and I would see people talking through the windows and I'd be like, what are they talking about?

289
00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:27.380
And I had the urge to like go out and like do what Michael did all the time, which was like, hey, what's going on?

290
00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:39.080
You know, like and it was like he was just so he knew so much about what he wanted out of the show that he would he would practice not just the skill of writing the characters,

291
00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:43.060
but the the skill of like embodying them or emotionally connecting to them.

292
00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.220
And it was just that's what I mean when I say it was professorial.

293
00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:49.600
It was it wasn't just like, all right, what's episode two?

294
00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:50.700
What's the act break?

295
00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:54.120
What's the first act break for the for the season finale?

296
00:16:54.120 --> 00:17:04.780
It was really like we had to embody the characters and understand them emotionally and and and socially in a way that I think led to great episodes.

297
00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:09.900
You know, well, it's so interesting because when all the actors got there, then they did that to us, too.

298
00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.460
They made us sit at our desks and just sit there and feel it.

299
00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.260
We've talked about that before.

300
00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:20.580
And so I love that that started all the way back with you guys writing the episodes.

301
00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:21.340
Yeah.

302
00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:25.420
June of June of 2004, like they're right from the from the jump.

303
00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:32.400
There were things like that that we did that weren't that weren't the typical things that you would do with a writing staff.

304
00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:37.580
Another thing he did all the time, which I thought was so smart, which I still do on shows that I work on now,

305
00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:48.840
is we would come into work one day and he would he would have he would put all the characters names on a board and he would pick two of them at random and he would give them to he would say me and Larry Wilmore,

306
00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:53.480
like, go come up with five story ideas for Oscar and Kelly.

307
00:17:54.140 --> 00:18:02.440
And then and then you, Mindy and Paul, like go come up with five story ideas for, you know, Jim and Stanley.

308
00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:09.760
And for an hour, we would just pitch, OK, well, what the hell could a story be with Oscar and Kelly?

309
00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:12.380
But it led to some really cool stuff.

310
00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:23.320
And like some of those some of the little the B stories and the C stories of of that came that were in the first couple of seasons were just brainstorming like random pairings of people.

311
00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:31.460
He he understood fundamentally that one of the great advantages that the show had over other shows is that we had 20 people.

312
00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:36.420
We had like most shows have whatever, six main characters or something.

313
00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:42.980
And so pretty quickly, you get tired of a story with involving these two people.

314
00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:44.020
There are these three people.

315
00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.640
And he was like, that's not what this is going to be.

316
00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:56.660
This is going to be the whole point of the show is to observe like an anthropologist, observe the comings and goings of a large number of people in the same office environment.

317
00:18:56.660 --> 00:19:02.760
So like go work on random pairings and like sometimes really fun stuff would come out of it, you know,

318
00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:13.520
like he just so thoroughly understood what what made the show special and what the potential was for how good it could be that he had all of these machinations.

319
00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:18.840
And he was moved, always moving pieces around the chessboard with the writers in those for in those early days, you know?

320
00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:28.980
Yeah, I remember telling my dad, I was trying to describe this group of people and the show to my dad, who was not at all in entertainment, you know, he was a drilling engineer.

321
00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:31.800
But sometimes we could talk sports.

322
00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:33.520
And I said, Dad, here's the deal.

323
00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:35.520
We have a deep bench, Dad.

324
00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:38.800
And it's like a basketball term, like our team is so good.

325
00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.780
There's so many layers to this team.

326
00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:48.980
And then when he came and, you know, Greg invited them to sit at a table read at lunch, my dad was like, I get it.

327
00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:49.480
I get it.

328
00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:49.820
I get it now.

329
00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:50.460
Yeah, I get it.

330
00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:54.600
Like, because there were laughs, I think, coming from him and all these places, you know?

331
00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:55.480
Yeah.

332
00:19:55.800 --> 00:20:05.980
Well, I was going to say that in season nine, John and I were invited to be producers on the show to kind of help carve out Jim and Pam's final season.

333
00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:10.660
And we got to come into their writer's room, mostly as observers.

334
00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:16.380
And Greg was running the show that season, and he was still doing those exercises.

335
00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.380
And it was fascinating.

336
00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:22.300
Except in this case, it was more like homework.

337
00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:27.960
I remember the day started, and Greg said, OK, get out your homework.

338
00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:28.500
What?

339
00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:38.640
And the homework had been that everyone had to come up with five ideas for, I can't remember what it was, but it was like for a Dwight and Stanley cold open.

340
00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:39.400
Go.

341
00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:43.720
And then you would just sit and listen.

342
00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:49.920
And then when there was an idea that piqued his interest, he would say, oh, oh, OK, you two, go off, flesh that out.

343
00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:54.160
And then two people would leave the room, and then we would keep listening to the homework.

344
00:20:54.160 --> 00:21:03.280
And I remember thinking, oh, my gosh, I would be so terrified to present my five ideas, because sometimes it was just crickets.

345
00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.820
Like, you could just hear the ideas just die in the room.

346
00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:19.800
I mean, what an intense and vulnerable thing to pitch your creative ideas in front of, like, a room of people who are all judging whether or not you should follow that thread.

347
00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:26.980
Yes, one of his greatest strengths is that he is unafraid, I would say, of failure.

348
00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:37.160
And I think, I believe that that comes, I'm guessing that that comes partially from working at Saturday Night Live, because what you just described of, like,

349
00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:40.180
pitching ideas and having them bomb, right?

350
00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:46.080
That is the essence of working at SNL, except your failures at SNL are public.

351
00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:54.740
Like, you write sketches, and there's a read-through with the entire cast of the show and Lauren and all the producers around a giant table.

352
00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:59.840
And the audience is 150 people who work on the show.

353
00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:07.440
And, by the way, a very famous person who's hosting the show, like, you know, Drake is reading your sketch or whatever.

354
00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:07.960
Oh, my God.

355
00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:13.800
And there are moments at SNL where, you know, your sketches are 12 pages long or whatever.

356
00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:19.860
And your sketch starts, and you get to page two, and, like, the joke of the sketch is sprung, and no one laughs.

357
00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:23.260
And in your head, you're like, there's 10 more pages of this.

358
00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:27.200
Like, we have to sit here for 10 more pages as no one finds my premise funny.

359
00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:30.840
And so, for the first – I don't know what Greg's experience was.

360
00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:32.680
I've never really talked to him about his time there.

361
00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:39.440
But for the first, like, six months, you fall into a deep depression.

362
00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:44.000
Like, it's just, like, it's so embarrassing to fail that way publicly.

363
00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:47.960
And then the week goes by, and the show happens.

364
00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.640
And then it's like, all right, it's Monday again.

365
00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:50.140
Do it again.

366
00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:51.660
And you, like, do it again.

367
00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:52.740
And you fail again.

368
00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:53.780
And you fail.

369
00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:54.440
And you fail.

370
00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:54.980
And you fail.

371
00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:59.220
And eventually, at some point, hopefully, you kind of figure it out.

372
00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:01.040
And you write a sketch, and it gets some laughs.

373
00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.200
And you're like, okay, it's not the end of the world.

374
00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:04.100
Like, I'm still here.

375
00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:06.320
And then you get a little better and a little better.

376
00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:11.720
And that, like, that theory is – it's pretty brutal on your ego.

377
00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:20.280
But when you've already had it driven out of your ego from working at SNL, like, five Stanley cold opens, I can – who cares?

378
00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:20.740
No problem.

379
00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:21.620
Yeah, exactly.

380
00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:26.580
So, I – that – and his theory is really, like, this is a numbers game.

381
00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:32.560
Like, the way to get to the best ideas is you have to create a thousand ideas in order to find the two that are good.

382
00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.000
And he took that approach with everything.

383
00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:36.780
He took that approach when he was hiring writers.

384
00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.620
He took that approach when he was casting people.

385
00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:43.020
I mean, how long did the casting process go on for the show?

386
00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:44.340
Endlessly long.

387
00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:45.740
Endlessly, endlessly long.

388
00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:51.340
And that's just his – he's just the most thorough person when it comes to that.

389
00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:52.680
He doesn't settle for anything.

390
00:23:53.020 --> 00:24:05.080
He demands that you walk down – I mean, when we're – when we were breaking stories, like, we would get five-sixths of the way down the line toward breaking a story.

391
00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:08.560
And we would get to a point where he would realize it didn't work, and he would go, like, man, this doesn't work.

392
00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:09.040
Throw it away.

393
00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:09.540
Start over.

394
00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.020
And it's really exhausting.

395
00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:15.200
But also, the show lasted for ten years.

396
00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:15.460
Yeah.

397
00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:16.740
And it was really good.

398
00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:17.260
So –

399
00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:19.520
And here we are still talking about it.

400
00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:19.940
Yeah.

401
00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:20.520
Exactly.

402
00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:20.880
Yeah.

403
00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:22.000
Seventeen years later.

404
00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:22.740
Wow.

405
00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:35.080
Well, now I think we have to ask you, Mike.

406
00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:36.100
Yeah, we do.

407
00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:38.580
How did you come to play Moe's?

408
00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:39.040
Yeah.

409
00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:41.240
Something we understand you hated.

410
00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.580
You hated being Moe's, but we love Moe's.

411
00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:48.460
Let me just delineate here importantly.

412
00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:51.820
I love the fact that I played Moe's.

413
00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.800
I hated actually doing it.

414
00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:55.320
Okay.

415
00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:57.820
For two reasons.

416
00:24:58.000 --> 00:24:59.160
So here's the story.

417
00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:05.280
The story was in the – Greg always had it as part of Dwight Schrute's backstory,

418
00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:13.740
that he himself was not Amish or Mennonite or anything like that, but he was sort of Amish

419
00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:15.540
adjacent, that he had relatives.

420
00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:20.440
He was a Germanic – he was of Germanic descent living in Pennsylvania.

421
00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:25.000
That is – that alone is sort of Amish adjacent.

422
00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:30.440
So Greg always had it in the back of his head that there was a sort of cousin – a

423
00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:37.040
shrewd cousin farming community that was either Amish or something in the Amish family.

424
00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:45.300
So in 2004, there was a reality show called Amish in the City.

425
00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:46.240
Yes.

426
00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:47.480
Yes, I saw that.

427
00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:48.300
Yes.

428
00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:51.060
It was a terrible, terrible program.

429
00:25:51.080 --> 00:25:52.560
It was so hard to watch.

430
00:25:52.560 --> 00:26:01.260
It was so awful, and I – my wife and I watched it, and I was sort of trying to express how

431
00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.560
cringey it was to the writers' room one day.

432
00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.720
The premise of the show was they took a bunch of idiot club kids from L.A.

433
00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:10.440
who were on spring

434
00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:14.740
break, and they were like, you guys are going to come stay in this awesome house in the Hollywood

435
00:26:14.740 --> 00:26:15.120
Hills.

436
00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:19.020
And they were psyched, and they were like, we're going to get so drunk and party and whatever.

437
00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:25.080
What they didn't know that also invited to stay in the house were a bunch of young Amish

438
00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:31.880
folks on Rumspringa who had been released for their year of exploration, and they were going

439
00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:32.560
to live together.

440
00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:35.940
And then, you know –

441
00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:36.880
Hot mess ensues.

442
00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:38.640
Hot mess ensues, right.

443
00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:45.460
So, one of the characters on the show who was Amish was named Mose, and Mose was this

444
00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:55.360
delightful, kind-hearted, fragile egg who tried his best to become friends with the idiot

445
00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.520
club kids from L.A.

446
00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:03.660
And among other ways that he tried this were he fashioned his own wooden toys, like a little

447
00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:05.540
wooden airplane or a little wooden car.

448
00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:08.280
He made them himself.

449
00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:12.600
He made them himself, and he presented them to his new friends, and they were just like,

450
00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:13.840
what the hell is this, right?

451
00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:20.620
And so, I was, Rick, relating this story to the room about how awful this show was, and

452
00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:25.600
Greg pointed at me with his giant finger – I remember it so clearly – and said, you're

453
00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:26.440
going to play Mose.

454
00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:27.440
You're Dwight's cousin.

455
00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:28.540
